      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_6",
      "quote": "A MedDiet has been associated with improved metabolic syndrome (MetS) outcomes, including a decreased risk of type 2 diabetes, cardiovascular disease and obesity, among adults. However, the extent to which different levels of adherence to the MedDiet are associated with MetS outcomes in youth remains poorly understood. Several studies have investigated the associations between adherence to a MedDiet and MetS outcomes among adolescents, 21-26 but the results have been inconsistent.",
      "reason": "Summarizes existing adult evidence and prior adolescent studies, and explicitly notes key uncertainties (poorly understood associations and inconsistent findings in youth)."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_6",
      "quote": "The prevalence of metabolic disorders is increasing globally, especially among adolescents, and this has become a public health concern. To our knowledge, no previous systematic review or meta- analysis has synthesised the available evidence examining the association between adherence to the MedDiet and MetS among children and adolescents. Therefore, the aim of this systematic review and meta- analysis was to investigate whether adherence to a MedDiet is associated with MetS in this population.",
      "reason": "Explains the public health importance and the need for this review due to the absence of prior syntheses focused on children/adolescents."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e3",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "The authors state that no previous systematic review or meta-analysis has synthesized this association in children/adolescents, so explaining overlap with similar reviews does not apply."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e4",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "This is not a review of intervention effects; eligibility included observational longitudinal and cross-sectional designs only."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e5",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "A logic model for complex interventions is not applicable because the review synthesizes observational associations rather than complex intervention delivery."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 88
